{"name":"Mountain Mist","hex":"#a09f9c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevation of land of considerable dimensions rising more or less abruptly, forming a conspicuous figure in the landscape, usually having a small extent of surface at its summit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something very large in size or quantity; a huge amount; a great heap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain"},{"term":"mist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water or other liquid finely suspended in air.","A layer of fine droplets or particles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form mist.","To spray fine droplets on, particularly of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"},{"title":"Mist","blocks":[{"text":"Mist is a natural phenomenon caused by small droplets of water aerosols suspended in the cold air, usually by condensation. Physically, it is an example of a dispersion, most commonly seen where water vapor in warm, moist air meets sudden cooling, such as in exhaled air in the winter, or when hot sauna steam is suddenly released outside. Mist occurs naturally as part of weather, typically when humid air comes into contact with surfaces that are much cooler. It can also be created artificially with aerosol spray dispensers if the humidity and temperature conditions are right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mist","wikidata":"Q192196"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chimborazo, Ecuador, whose summit is the point farthest away from the Earth's center[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVolc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png/500px-Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/th